  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA weather pattern change is on the way beginning this weekend. Long-\nrange guidance is in agreement with upper-level troughing near the \nregion beginning on Saturday bringing abundant moisture and lift \nyielding appreciable rain chances the region has not seen in a \nwhile. The highest chances (50-70%) remain focused over portions \nof southeast New Mexico and the western higher terrain, where the \nmost favorable moisture and lift are anticipated. Areas elsewhere \nwill generally have a low to medium (20-50%) chance of \nshowers/storms especially on Saturday night. PWATs exceeding the \n90th climatological percentile suggests the threat of heavy \nrainfall leading to localized flash flooding. This will be \nsomething to monitor at least this weekend. Lowered heights \nassociated with the upper trough and increased cloud cover will \nalso cool temperatures gradually heading into next week. Model \nguidance still suggests uncertainty when temperatures truly cool \ndown. Ensembles are starting to trend towards Tuesday, where \nhighs are forecast to be in the 80s to low 90s. However, these \ncooler temperatures will depend on the position of the cold front \nand trough. \n\nLamberson",
